## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to simplify tagging, maintain per-env isolation, and introduce a safe migration path.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform provider default_tags
- Per-env backend prefixes
- Gate checks on plan output for tag presence
- Migration risk and rollback considerations

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  default_tags = {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
    }
  }
}
```

```hcl
# migrated resource sample (no wrapper)
resource "aws_vpc" "main" { cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16" }
```

```hcl
# backend example
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "corp-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle prod tag overrides if needed?
- What tooling would you add to enforce tag presence across environments?
